{"subjectId":"dsa-advanced","id":"infix_postfix","title":"Infix, Postfix, Prefix","group":"Linked Lists, Stacks & Queues","order":11,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Humans write <code>x + y</code>; machines prefer <code>x y +</code> or <code>+ x y</code>, because those forms need no brackets, no precedence table and no associativity rules — a single left-to-right pass with a stack evaluates them. Converting between the three notations is the classic stack algorithm.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Infix / Postfix / Prefix</strong> — the three ways to place an operator relative to its operands: <strong>infix</strong> between them (<code>x + y</code>), <strong>postfix</strong> after them (<code>x y +</code>, also called <em>Reverse Polish Notation</em>), <strong>prefix</strong> before them (<code>+ x y</code>, <em>Polish Notation</em>).</p></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Infix</th><th>Postfix</th><th>Prefix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>x + y</code></td><td><code>x y +</code></td><td><code>+ x y</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>x + y * z</code></td><td><code>x y z * +</code></td><td><code>+ x * y z</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(x + y) * z</code></td><td><code>x y + z *</code></td><td><code>* + x y z</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>a + b * (c - d) / e</code></td><td><code>a b c d - * e / +</code></td><td><code>+ a / * b - c d e</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>a ^ b ^ c</code> (right-assoc)</td><td><code>a b c ^ ^</code></td><td><code>^ a ^ b c</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bulb\"></i>Why postfix and prefix are useful</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets-off\"></i>No parentheses</h3><p>The position of each operator already says which operands it applies to, so brackets are never needed. <code>(x + y) * z</code> is just <code>x y + z *</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>No precedence or associativity rules</h3><p>Those rules only exist to disambiguate infix. Once an expression is in postfix or prefix, <code>*</code> before <code>+</code> is decided by position, not by a table.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>One pass to evaluate</h3><p>A single left-to-right scan with one stack evaluates postfix (right-to-left for prefix). Compilers and calculators (HP's RPN models, the JVM's stack machine, PostScript) run on exactly this.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary-tree\"></i>Same tree, three traversals</h3><p>All three notations are traversals of the same <em>expression tree</em>: prefix = preorder, infix = inorder, postfix = postorder. Converting is just re-traversing.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>The expression tree of a + b * c and its three traversals</figcaption><pre><code>   +                    preorder  (prefix)  : + a * b c\n /   \\                  inorder   (infix)   : a + b * c\na     *                 postorder (postfix) : a b c * +\n     / \\\n    b   c</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>Precedence and associativity</h3>\n<p>Infix is ambiguous without two extra rules. <strong>Precedence</strong> decides which operator binds tighter when they differ; <strong>associativity</strong> decides the order when they are equal.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Precedence</th><th>Associativity</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>^</code></td><td>3 (highest)</td><td>right to left</td><td><code>2 ^ 1 ^ 2 = 2 ^ (1 ^ 2) = 2 ^ 1 = 2</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>*</code>, <code>/</code></td><td>2</td><td>left to right</td><td><code>12 / 2 * 3 = (12 / 2) * 3 = 18</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>+</code>, <code>-</code></td><td>1 (lowest)</td><td>left to right</td><td><code>10 + 2 - 3 = (10 + 2) - 3 = 9</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Precedence:</strong> <code>10 + 20 * 2</code> → <code>10 + 40</code> → <code>50</code>, not <code>60</code>.</li><li><strong>Left associativity:</strong> <code>10 - 2 - 3</code> → <code>(10 - 2) - 3</code> → <code>5</code>, not <code>11</code>.</li><li><strong>Right associativity:</strong> <code>2 ^ 3 ^ 2</code> → <code>2 ^ (3 ^ 2)</code> → <code>2 ^ 9</code> → <code>512</code>, not <code>64</code>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">How the rules enter the algorithm</div><p>In the conversion algorithms, precedence decides whether an incoming operator pops the stack top; associativity decides what happens when they are <strong>equal</strong>: left-associative operators pop the equal top (so the earlier one is output first), right-associative ones do not.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-pencil\"></i>Converting by hand: fully parenthesise, then move operators</h3>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Parenthesise fully</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Add brackets that make precedence and associativity explicit: <code>x + y * z</code> → <code>(x + (y * z))</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Convert innermost first</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Replace each <code>(a op b)</code> by <code>a b op</code> (postfix) or <code>op a b</code> (prefix), working outwards: <code>(x + (y z *))</code> → <code>x y z * +</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Drop the brackets</div><div class=\"step-desc\">They are no longer needed — the result is the postfix (or prefix) form.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Infix</th><th>Fully parenthesised</th><th>Postfix</th><th>Prefix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>x + y * z</code></td><td><code>(x + (y * z))</code></td><td><code>x y z * +</code></td><td><code>+ x * y z</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(x + y) * z</code></td><td><code>((x + y) * z)</code></td><td><code>x y + z *</code></td><td><code>* + x y z</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>a - b - c</code></td><td><code>((a - b) - c)</code></td><td><code>a b - c -</code></td><td><code>- - a b c</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>a ^ b ^ c</code></td><td><code>(a ^ (b ^ c))</code></td><td><code>a b c ^ ^</code></td><td><code>^ a ^ b c</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-right\"></i>Infix to postfix (shunting-yard algorithm)</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>a+b*c</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>abc*+</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>(a+b)*c</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>ab+c*</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>a+b*(c-d)/e</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>abcd-*e/+</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Operands go straight to the output; they are already in the right order. Operators wait on a stack until every operator that must be applied <em>before</em> them has been output. An operator on the stack must come out before an incoming operator of lower (or equal, for left-associative) precedence — that is the only rule. Brackets are a fence: <code>(</code> is pushed and nothing below it can be popped until the matching <code>)</code> arrives.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Operand</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Append it to the output.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>(</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">Push it.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>)</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">Pop and output operators until the <code>(</code>; pop the <code>(</code> and discard it (brackets never appear in postfix).</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Operator <code>x</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">While the stack top is an operator with <strong>higher</strong> precedence than <code>x</code>, or <strong>equal</strong> precedence and <code>x</code> is left-associative, pop it to the output. Then push <code>x</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">5</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">End of input</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Pop and output everything left on the stack.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Action</th><th>Stack (bottom → top)</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>a</code></td><td>operand → output</td><td></td><td><code>a</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>+</code></td><td>stack empty → push</td><td><code>+</code></td><td><code>a</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>b</code></td><td>operand → output</td><td><code>+</code></td><td><code>a b</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>*</code></td><td>higher than <code>+</code> → push</td><td><code>+ *</code></td><td><code>a b</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(</code></td><td>push</td><td><code>+ * (</code></td><td><code>a b</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>c</code></td><td>operand → output</td><td><code>+ * (</code></td><td><code>a b c</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>-</code></td><td>top is <code>(</code> → push</td><td><code>+ * ( -</code></td><td><code>a b c</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>d</code></td><td>operand → output</td><td><code>+ * ( -</code></td><td><code>a b c d</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>)</code></td><td>pop to <code>(</code>: output <code>-</code>, discard <code>(</code></td><td><code>+ *</code></td><td><code>a b c d -</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>/</code></td><td>equal to <code>*</code>, left-assoc → pop <code>*</code>; <code>+</code> is lower → stop; push <code>/</code></td><td><code>+ /</code></td><td><code>a b c d - *</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>e</code></td><td>operand → output</td><td><code>+ /</code></td><td><code>a b c d - * e</code></td></tr><tr><td>end</td><td>pop everything</td><td></td><td><code>a b c d - * e / +</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>PREC = {'+': 1, '-': 1, '*': 2, '/': 2, '^': 3}\nRIGHT_ASSOC = {'^'}\n\ndef infix_to_postfix(expr):\n    out, st = [], []\n    for ch in expr:\n        if ch == ' ':\n            continue\n        if ch.isalnum():                       # operand: straight to the output\n            out.append(ch)\n        elif ch == '(':\n            st.append(ch)\n        elif ch == ')':\n            while st[-1] != '(':               # pop until the matching '('\n                out.append(st.pop())\n            st.pop()                           # discard the '(' itself\n        else:                                  # operator\n            while (st and st[-1] != '(' and\n                   (PREC[st[-1]] &gt; PREC[ch] or\n                    (PREC[st[-1]] == PREC[ch] and ch not in RIGHT_ASSOC))):\n                out.append(st.pop())           # higher (or equal, left-assoc) comes out first\n            st.append(ch)\n    while st:\n        out.append(st.pop())\n    return ''.join(out)\n\nfor e in [\"a+b*c\", \"(a+b)*c\", \"a+b*(c-d)/e\", \"a^b^c\", \"a-b-c\"]:\n    print(e, \"→\", infix_to_postfix(e))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cctype&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stack&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint prec(char op) {\n    if (op == '^') return 3;\n    if (op == '*' || op == '/') return 2;\n    if (op == '+' || op == '-') return 1;\n    return 0;\n}\nbool rightAssoc(char op) { return op == '^'; }\n\nstring infixToPostfix(const string&amp; expr) {\n    string out;\n    stack&lt;char&gt; st;\n    for (char ch : expr) {\n        if (ch == ' ') continue;\n        if (isalnum(static_cast&lt;unsigned char&gt;(ch))) {\n            out += ch;                                   // operand: straight to the output\n        } else if (ch == '(') {\n            st.push(ch);\n        } else if (ch == ')') {\n            while (st.top() != '(') { out += st.top(); st.pop(); }   // pop until the matching '('\n            st.pop();                                    // discard the '(' itself\n        } else {\n            while (!st.empty() &amp;&amp; st.top() != '(' &amp;&amp;\n                   (prec(st.top()) &gt; prec(ch) || (prec(st.top()) == prec(ch) &amp;&amp; !rightAssoc(ch)))) {\n                out += st.top(); st.pop();               // higher (or equal, left-assoc) comes out first\n            }\n            st.push(ch);\n        }\n    }\n    while (!st.empty()) { out += st.top(); st.pop(); }\n    return out;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    for (string e : {\"a+b*c\", \"(a+b)*c\", \"a+b*(c-d)/e\", \"a^b^c\", \"a-b-c\"})\n        cout &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; \" → \" &lt;&lt; infixToPostfix(e) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static int prec(char op) {\n        if (op == '^') return 3;\n        if (op == '*' || op == '/') return 2;\n        if (op == '+' || op == '-') return 1;\n        return 0;\n    }\n    static boolean rightAssoc(char op) { return op == '^'; }\n\n    static String infixToPostfix(String expr) {\n        StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();\n        Deque&lt;Character&gt; st = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        for (char ch : expr.toCharArray()) {\n            if (ch == ' ') continue;\n            if (Character.isLetterOrDigit(ch)) {\n                out.append(ch);                              // operand: straight to the output\n            } else if (ch == '(') {\n                st.push(ch);\n            } else if (ch == ')') {\n                while (st.peek() != '(') out.append(st.pop());   // pop until the matching '('\n                st.pop();                                    // discard the '(' itself\n            } else {\n                while (!st.isEmpty() &amp;&amp; st.peek() != '(' &amp;&amp;\n                       (prec(st.peek()) &gt; prec(ch) || (prec(st.peek()) == prec(ch) &amp;&amp; !rightAssoc(ch))))\n                    out.append(st.pop());                    // higher (or equal, left-assoc) comes out first\n                st.push(ch);\n            }\n        }\n        while (!st.isEmpty()) out.append(st.pop());\n        return out.toString();\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (String e : new String[]{\"a+b*c\", \"(a+b)*c\", \"a+b*(c-d)/e\", \"a^b^c\", \"a-b-c\"})\n            System.out.println(e + \" → \" + infixToPostfix(e));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const PREC = { '+': 1, '-': 1, '*': 2, '/': 2, '^': 3 };\nconst RIGHT_ASSOC = new Set(['^']);\nconst isOperand = ch =&gt; /[A-Za-z0-9]/.test(ch);\n\nfunction infixToPostfix(expr) {\n    const out = [], st = [];\n    const top = () =&gt; st[st.length - 1];\n    for (const ch of expr) {\n        if (ch === ' ') continue;\n        if (isOperand(ch)) {\n            out.push(ch);                                   // operand: straight to the output\n        } else if (ch === '(') {\n            st.push(ch);\n        } else if (ch === ')') {\n            while (top() !== '(') out.push(st.pop());       // pop until the matching '('\n            st.pop();                                       // discard the '(' itself\n        } else {\n            while (st.length &amp;&amp; top() !== '(' &amp;&amp;\n                   (PREC[top()] &gt; PREC[ch] || (PREC[top()] === PREC[ch] &amp;&amp; !RIGHT_ASSOC.has(ch))))\n                out.push(st.pop());                         // higher (or equal, left-assoc) comes out first\n            st.push(ch);\n        }\n    }\n    while (st.length) out.push(st.pop());\n    return out.join('');\n}\n\nfor (const e of [\"a+b*c\", \"(a+b)*c\", \"a+b*(c-d)/e\", \"a^b^c\", \"a-b-c\"])\n    console.log(e, \"→\", infixToPostfix(e));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>a+b*c → abc*+\n(a+b)*c → ab+c*\na+b*(c-d)/e → abcd-*e/+\na^b^c → abc^^\na-b-c → ab-c-</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) for the operator stack</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Three places where this goes wrong: (1) comparing precedence with a <code>(</code> on top — give <code>(</code> the lowest precedence or test for it explicitly, otherwise <code>)</code> never finds its partner; (2) using <code>&gt;</code> instead of <code>&gt;=</code> for left-associative operators, which turns <code>a-b-c</code> into <code>abc--</code> = <code>a-(b-c)</code>; (3) the <code>)</code> case must <em>discard</em> the <code>(</code> — brackets never reach the output.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calculator\"></i>Evaluating a postfix expression</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>10 2 * 3 +</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>23</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>2 3 1 * + 9 -</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>-4</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>100 20 5 / -</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>96</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Scan left to right. Operands are pushed. When an operator arrives, its two operands are the two most recent values — pop them, apply, push the result. The expression is well formed exactly when one value remains at the end. <strong>Order matters for <code>-</code>, <code>/</code> and <code>^</code>:</strong> the first pop is the <em>right</em> operand.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Operand</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Push it.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Operator</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>right = pop()</code>, <code>left = pop()</code>, push <code>left op right</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">End</div><div class=\"step-desc\">The single remaining value is the result.</div></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Each operator replaces the top two values by one</figcaption><pre><code>tokens:  10   2   *   3   +\nstack:  [10] [10 2] [20] [20 3] [23]        2 3 1 * + 9 -  →  [2] [2 3] [2 3 1] [2 3] [5] [5 9] [-4]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def apply(op, a, b):\n    if op == '+': return a + b\n    if op == '-': return a - b\n    if op == '*': return a * b\n    if op == '/': return a // b           # integer division (operands kept positive here)\n    if op == '^': return a ** b\n    raise ValueError(op)\n\ndef eval_postfix(expr):\n    st = []\n    for tok in expr.split():\n        if tok.lstrip('-').isdigit():\n            st.append(int(tok))\n        else:\n            right = st.pop()              # first pop is the RIGHT operand\n            left = st.pop()\n            st.append(apply(tok, left, right))\n    return st[-1]                         # exactly one value remains\n\nfor e in [\"10 2 * 3 +\", \"2 3 1 * + 9 -\", \"100 20 5 / -\", \"2 3 ^ 1 -\"]:\n    print(e, \"=\", eval_postfix(e))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;sstream&gt;\n#include &lt;stack&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nlong long ipow(long long a, long long b) { long long r = 1; while (b-- &gt; 0) r *= a; return r; }\n\nlong long apply(const string&amp; op, long long a, long long b) {\n    if (op == \"+\") return a + b;\n    if (op == \"-\") return a - b;\n    if (op == \"*\") return a * b;\n    if (op == \"/\") return a / b;         // integer division (operands kept positive here)\n    return ipow(a, b);                   // \"^\"\n}\n\nlong long evalPostfix(const string&amp; expr) {\n    stack&lt;long long&gt; st;\n    istringstream in(expr);\n    string tok;\n    while (in &gt;&gt; tok) {\n        if (isdigit(static_cast&lt;unsigned char&gt;(tok.back()))) {\n            st.push(stoll(tok));\n        } else {\n            long long right = st.top(); st.pop();   // first pop is the RIGHT operand\n            long long left = st.top(); st.pop();\n            st.push(apply(tok, left, right));\n        }\n    }\n    return st.top();                                // exactly one value remains\n}\n\nint main() {\n    for (string e : {\"10 2 * 3 +\", \"2 3 1 * + 9 -\", \"100 20 5 / -\", \"2 3 ^ 1 -\"})\n        cout &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; \" = \" &lt;&lt; evalPostfix(e) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static long ipow(long a, long b) { long r = 1; while (b-- &gt; 0) r *= a; return r; }\n\n    static long apply(String op, long a, long b) {\n        switch (op) {\n            case \"+\": return a + b;\n            case \"-\": return a - b;\n            case \"*\": return a * b;\n            case \"/\": return a / b;          // integer division (operands kept positive here)\n            default:  return ipow(a, b);     // \"^\"\n        }\n    }\n\n    static long evalPostfix(String expr) {\n        Deque&lt;Long&gt; st = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        for (String tok : expr.split(\" \")) {\n            if (Character.isDigit(tok.charAt(tok.length() - 1))) {\n                st.push(Long.parseLong(tok));\n            } else {\n                long right = st.pop();           // first pop is the RIGHT operand\n                long left = st.pop();\n                st.push(apply(tok, left, right));\n            }\n        }\n        return st.peek();                        // exactly one value remains\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (String e : new String[]{\"10 2 * 3 +\", \"2 3 1 * + 9 -\", \"100 20 5 / -\", \"2 3 ^ 1 -\"})\n            System.out.println(e + \" = \" + evalPostfix(e));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function apply(op, a, b) {\n    switch (op) {\n        case '+': return a + b;\n        case '-': return a - b;\n        case '*': return a * b;\n        case '/': return Math.trunc(a / b);     // integer division (operands kept positive here)\n        case '^': return a ** b;\n    }\n    throw new Error(op);\n}\n\nfunction evalPostfix(expr) {\n    const st = [];\n    for (const tok of expr.split(' ')) {\n        if (/\\d$/.test(tok)) {\n            st.push(Number(tok));\n        } else {\n            const right = st.pop();              // first pop is the RIGHT operand\n            const left = st.pop();\n            st.push(apply(tok, left, right));\n        }\n    }\n    return st[st.length - 1];                    // exactly one value remains\n}\n\nfor (const e of [\"10 2 * 3 +\", \"2 3 1 * + 9 -\", \"100 20 5 / -\", \"2 3 ^ 1 -\"])\n    console.log(e, \"=\", evalPostfix(e));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 2 * 3 + = 23\n2 3 1 * + 9 - = -4\n100 20 5 / - = 96\n2 3 ^ 1 - = 7</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) for the operand stack</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p><code>op1 = pop(); op2 = pop(); push(op2 op op1)</code> — the original notes wrote it correctly, but swapping the two is the single most common bug: <code>10 2 -</code> must give <code>8</code>, not <code>-8</code>. Also tokenise on spaces when operands can have several digits; a character-by-character scan silently reads <code>10</code> as <code>1</code> and <code>0</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-left\"></i>Infix to prefix</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>x+y*z</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>+x*yz</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>(a+b)*c</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>*+abc</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>a+b*(c-d)/e</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>+a/*b-cde</code></p></div>\n<p>By hand: fully parenthesise and convert from the innermost bracket outwards, writing each operator <em>before</em> its operands — <code>x + (y * z)</code> → <code>x + (* y z)</code> → <code>+ x * y z</code>. With a stack there are two routes; the reversal trick reuses the postfix code almost unchanged.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Prefix is almost <em>postfix read backwards</em>: reverse the infix string (swapping <code>(</code> with <code>)</code>), run the postfix algorithm, and reverse the result. The one subtlety is associativity, which flips with the reversal: in the reversed string a left-associative operator must <strong>not</strong> pop an equal-precedence top (otherwise <code>a-b-c</code> would come out as <code>-a-bc</code> = <code>a-(b-c)</code>), while the right-associative <code>^</code> must.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Reverse</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Reverse the infix string and swap every <code>(</code> with <code>)</code>: <code>(a+b)*c</code> → <code>c*(b+a)</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Convert</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Run infix-to-postfix on it, popping on <strong>strictly higher</strong> precedence (or equal precedence when the operator is right-associative): <code>c*(b+a)</code> → <code>cba+*</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Reverse again</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Reverse the output: <code>cba+*</code> → <code>*+abc</code>.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>PREC = {'+': 1, '-': 1, '*': 2, '/': 2, '^': 3}\nRIGHT_ASSOC = {'^'}\n\ndef infix_to_prefix(expr):\n    rev = ''.join(')' if c == '(' else '(' if c == ')' else c for c in reversed(expr))\n    out, st = [], []\n    for ch in rev:\n        if ch == ' ':\n            continue\n        if ch.isalnum():\n            out.append(ch)\n        elif ch == '(':\n            st.append(ch)\n        elif ch == ')':\n            while st[-1] != '(':\n                out.append(st.pop())\n            st.pop()\n        else:\n            while (st and st[-1] != '(' and\n                   (PREC[st[-1]] &gt; PREC[ch] or\n                    (PREC[st[-1]] == PREC[ch] and ch in RIGHT_ASSOC))):   # associativity flipped\n                out.append(st.pop())\n            st.append(ch)\n    while st:\n        out.append(st.pop())\n    return ''.join(reversed(out))                                       # undo the reversal\n\nfor e in [\"x+y*z\", \"(a+b)*c\", \"a+b*(c-d)/e\", \"a^b^c\", \"a-b-c\"]:\n    print(e, \"→\", infix_to_prefix(e))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;cctype&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stack&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint prec(char op) {\n    if (op == '^') return 3;\n    if (op == '*' || op == '/') return 2;\n    if (op == '+' || op == '-') return 1;\n    return 0;\n}\nbool rightAssoc(char op) { return op == '^'; }\n\nstring infixToPrefix(string expr) {\n    reverse(expr.begin(), expr.end());\n    for (char&amp; c : expr) c = c == '(' ? ')' : c == ')' ? '(' : c;   // swap the brackets\n    string out;\n    stack&lt;char&gt; st;\n    for (char ch : expr) {\n        if (ch == ' ') continue;\n        if (isalnum(static_cast&lt;unsigned char&gt;(ch))) {\n            out += ch;\n        } else if (ch == '(') {\n            st.push(ch);\n        } else if (ch == ')') {\n            while (st.top() != '(') { out += st.top(); st.pop(); }\n            st.pop();\n        } else {\n            while (!st.empty() &amp;&amp; st.top() != '(' &amp;&amp;\n                   (prec(st.top()) &gt; prec(ch) || (prec(st.top()) == prec(ch) &amp;&amp; rightAssoc(ch)))) {\n                out += st.top(); st.pop();                        // associativity flipped\n            }\n            st.push(ch);\n        }\n    }\n    while (!st.empty()) { out += st.top(); st.pop(); }\n    reverse(out.begin(), out.end());                              // undo the reversal\n    return out;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    for (string e : {\"x+y*z\", \"(a+b)*c\", \"a+b*(c-d)/e\", \"a^b^c\", \"a-b-c\"})\n        cout &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; \" → \" &lt;&lt; infixToPrefix(e) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static int prec(char op) {\n        if (op == '^') return 3;\n        if (op == '*' || op == '/') return 2;\n        if (op == '+' || op == '-') return 1;\n        return 0;\n    }\n    static boolean rightAssoc(char op) { return op == '^'; }\n\n    static String infixToPrefix(String expr) {\n        StringBuilder rev = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = expr.length() - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) {              // reverse and swap the brackets\n            char c = expr.charAt(i);\n            rev.append(c == '(' ? ')' : c == ')' ? '(' : c);\n        }\n        StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();\n        Deque&lt;Character&gt; st = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        for (char ch : rev.toString().toCharArray()) {\n            if (ch == ' ') continue;\n            if (Character.isLetterOrDigit(ch)) {\n                out.append(ch);\n            } else if (ch == '(') {\n                st.push(ch);\n            } else if (ch == ')') {\n                while (st.peek() != '(') out.append(st.pop());\n                st.pop();\n            } else {\n                while (!st.isEmpty() &amp;&amp; st.peek() != '(' &amp;&amp;\n                       (prec(st.peek()) &gt; prec(ch) || (prec(st.peek()) == prec(ch) &amp;&amp; rightAssoc(ch))))\n                    out.append(st.pop());                        // associativity flipped\n                st.push(ch);\n            }\n        }\n        while (!st.isEmpty()) out.append(st.pop());\n        return out.reverse().toString();                         // undo the reversal\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (String e : new String[]{\"x+y*z\", \"(a+b)*c\", \"a+b*(c-d)/e\", \"a^b^c\", \"a-b-c\"})\n            System.out.println(e + \" → \" + infixToPrefix(e));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const PREC = { '+': 1, '-': 1, '*': 2, '/': 2, '^': 3 };\nconst RIGHT_ASSOC = new Set(['^']);\nconst isOperand = ch =&gt; /[A-Za-z0-9]/.test(ch);\n\nfunction infixToPrefix(expr) {\n    const rev = [...expr].reverse().map(c =&gt; c === '(' ? ')' : c === ')' ? '(' : c);   // swap the brackets\n    const out = [], st = [];\n    const top = () =&gt; st[st.length - 1];\n    for (const ch of rev) {\n        if (ch === ' ') continue;\n        if (isOperand(ch)) {\n            out.push(ch);\n        } else if (ch === '(') {\n            st.push(ch);\n        } else if (ch === ')') {\n            while (top() !== '(') out.push(st.pop());\n            st.pop();\n        } else {\n            while (st.length &amp;&amp; top() !== '(' &amp;&amp;\n                   (PREC[top()] &gt; PREC[ch] || (PREC[top()] === PREC[ch] &amp;&amp; RIGHT_ASSOC.has(ch))))\n                out.push(st.pop());                              // associativity flipped\n            st.push(ch);\n        }\n    }\n    while (st.length) out.push(st.pop());\n    return out.reverse().join('');                               // undo the reversal\n}\n\nfor (const e of [\"x+y*z\", \"(a+b)*c\", \"a+b*(c-d)/e\", \"a^b^c\", \"a-b-c\"])\n    console.log(e, \"→\", infixToPrefix(e));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>x+y*z → +x*yz\n(a+b)*c → *+abc\na+b*(c-d)/e → +a/*b-cde\na^b^c → ^a^bc\na-b-c → --abc</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><strong>Direct route without reversal:</strong> scan the infix <em>right to left</em>, treating <code>)</code> as the opener and <code>(</code> as the closer, collect operands and popped operators into a list, and reverse that list at the end — this is exactly the step list in the original notes (push operands to <code>prefix</code>, push <code>)</code> to the stack, pop on <code>(</code>, compare precedence, finally reverse). The code above is the same algorithm with the reversal done up front, which lets it share its structure with the postfix converter.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calculator\"></i>Evaluating a prefix expression</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>+ 10 * 2 3</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>16</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>- + 7 * 4 5 2</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>25</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>/ 100 - 20 15</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>20</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Mirror the postfix evaluator: scan <strong>right to left</strong>. Operands are pushed; an operator pops two values, but now the first pop is the <strong>left</strong> operand (it was closer to the operator in the original text). Push the result; the last remaining value is the answer.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def apply(op, a, b):\n    if op == '+': return a + b\n    if op == '-': return a - b\n    if op == '*': return a * b\n    if op == '/': return a // b\n    raise ValueError(op)\n\ndef eval_prefix(expr):\n    st = []\n    for tok in reversed(expr.split()):      # right to left\n        if tok.isdigit():\n            st.append(int(tok))\n        else:\n            left = st.pop()                 # first pop is the LEFT operand this time\n            right = st.pop()\n            st.append(apply(tok, left, right))\n    return st[-1]\n\nfor e in [\"+ 10 * 2 3\", \"- + 7 * 4 5 2\", \"/ 100 - 20 15\"]:\n    print(e, \"=\", eval_prefix(e))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cctype&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;sstream&gt;\n#include &lt;stack&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nlong long apply(const string&amp; op, long long a, long long b) {\n    if (op == \"+\") return a + b;\n    if (op == \"-\") return a - b;\n    if (op == \"*\") return a * b;\n    return a / b;\n}\n\nlong long evalPrefix(const string&amp; expr) {\n    vector&lt;string&gt; toks;\n    istringstream in(expr);\n    for (string t; in &gt;&gt; t;) toks.push_back(t);\n    stack&lt;long long&gt; st;\n    for (int i = (int)toks.size() - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) {         // right to left\n        if (isdigit(static_cast&lt;unsigned char&gt;(toks[i].back()))) {\n            st.push(stoll(toks[i]));\n        } else {\n            long long left = st.top(); st.pop();              // first pop is the LEFT operand this time\n            long long right = st.top(); st.pop();\n            st.push(apply(toks[i], left, right));\n        }\n    }\n    return st.top();\n}\n\nint main() {\n    for (string e : {\"+ 10 * 2 3\", \"- + 7 * 4 5 2\", \"/ 100 - 20 15\"})\n        cout &lt;&lt; e &lt;&lt; \" = \" &lt;&lt; evalPrefix(e) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static long apply(String op, long a, long b) {\n        switch (op) {\n            case \"+\": return a + b;\n            case \"-\": return a - b;\n            case \"*\": return a * b;\n            default:  return a / b;\n        }\n    }\n\n    static long evalPrefix(String expr) {\n        String[] toks = expr.split(\" \");\n        Deque&lt;Long&gt; st = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        for (int i = toks.length - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) {              // right to left\n            if (Character.isDigit(toks[i].charAt(toks[i].length() - 1))) {\n                st.push(Long.parseLong(toks[i]));\n            } else {\n                long left = st.pop();                             // first pop is the LEFT operand this time\n                long right = st.pop();\n                st.push(apply(toks[i], left, right));\n            }\n        }\n        return st.peek();\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (String e : new String[]{\"+ 10 * 2 3\", \"- + 7 * 4 5 2\", \"/ 100 - 20 15\"})\n            System.out.println(e + \" = \" + evalPrefix(e));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function apply(op, a, b) {\n    switch (op) {\n        case '+': return a + b;\n        case '-': return a - b;\n        case '*': return a * b;\n        case '/': return Math.trunc(a / b);\n    }\n    throw new Error(op);\n}\n\nfunction evalPrefix(expr) {\n    const toks = expr.split(' ');\n    const st = [];\n    for (let i = toks.length - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) {                  // right to left\n        if (/\\d$/.test(toks[i])) {\n            st.push(Number(toks[i]));\n        } else {\n            const left = st.pop();                                // first pop is the LEFT operand this time\n            const right = st.pop();\n            st.push(apply(toks[i], left, right));\n        }\n    }\n    return st[st.length - 1];\n}\n\nfor (const e of [\"+ 10 * 2 3\", \"- + 7 * 4 5 2\", \"/ 100 - 20 15\"])\n    console.log(e, \"=\", evalPrefix(e));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>+ 10 * 2 3 = 16\n- + 7 * 4 5 2 = 25\n/ 100 - 20 15 = 20</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>The three notations side by side</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Infix</th><th>Postfix</th><th>Prefix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Operator position</td><td>between operands</td><td>after operands</td><td>before operands</td></tr><tr><td>Needs brackets / precedence</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td>Evaluation scan</td><td>needs two stacks or a parser</td><td>left → right, one stack</td><td>right → left, one stack</td></tr><tr><td>First pop on an operator is</td><td>—</td><td>the <strong>right</strong> operand</td><td>the <strong>left</strong> operand</td></tr><tr><td>Tree traversal</td><td>inorder</td><td>postorder</td><td>preorder</td></tr><tr><td>Used by</td><td>people, source code</td><td>JVM / CPython bytecode, PostScript, RPN calculators</td><td>Lisp, some compilers' IRs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview Q&amp;A</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why do compilers and virtual machines prefer postfix?</summary><div><p>Because it maps directly onto a stack machine: each operand becomes a <em>push</em>, each operator a <em>pop-pop-compute-push</em>. No lookahead, no precedence table, one pass. The parser deals with precedence once, when it converts the source expression; the runtime never sees it again.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How does the shunting-yard algorithm handle associativity?</summary><div><p>Only in the equal-precedence case. For a left-associative incoming operator, an equal-precedence top is popped first (so <code>a - b - c</code> → <code>a b - c -</code>); for a right-associative one (<code>^</code>) it stays, so the later operator is output first (<code>a ^ b ^ c</code> → <code>a b c ^ ^</code>).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can you evaluate an infix expression directly, without converting first?</summary><div><p>Yes — Dijkstra's two-stack algorithm: one stack for values and one for operators. It is the shunting-yard algorithm where, instead of <em>outputting</em> a popped operator, you immediately apply it to the top two values. The conversion and the evaluation are the same control flow with a different action.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is wrong with evaluating postfix character by character?</summary><div><p>Multi-digit numbers break: <code>10 2 *</code> would be read as <code>1</code>, <code>0</code>, <code>2</code>. Tokenise on spaces (or scan runs of digits) first. The same goes for identifiers longer than one character and for a unary minus, which needs its own token type.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How are the three notations related to the expression tree?</summary><div><p>They are its three depth-first traversals: preorder prints the operator first (prefix), inorder prints it between the subtrees (infix, which is why it needs brackets to recover the structure), postorder prints it last (postfix). Both prefix and postfix determine the tree uniquely; infix without brackets does not.</p></div></details></section>"}